From left to right the input pairs are `00`, `10`, `11`, `01`, `00` and `01`. The output is high only when the inputs differ.

Place the following Arduino Mega 2560 program segments inside `setup()`, `loop()`, or separate functions, with `Serial.begin(9600)` before printing. Use `PINx` to sample a pin. Use `PORTx` to read or modify the output latch.

```cpp
// (i) Sample PB3. Supply a defined external logic level.
DDRB &= ~(1<<DDB3);
PORTB &= ~(1<<PORTB3);
unsigned char pb3 = (PINB & (1<<PINB3)) != 0;

// (ii) Set PA2, clear PA5 and preserve the other latch bits.
PORTA = (PORTA | (1<<PORTA2)) & ~(1<<PORTA5);
DDRA |= (1<<DDA2) | (1<<DDA5);

// (iii) Shift the PORTC latch left by a program-selected amount.
unsigned char shift = 3;
unsigned char previous = PORTC;
PORTC = (shift < 8) ? (unsigned char)(previous << shift) : 0;

// (iv) Place ASCII '6' (0x36) on the eight PORTA outputs.
PORTA = '6';
DDRA = 0xFF;

// (v) Place the commands to be measured between timestamps.
DDRB |= (1<<DDB7);    // Configure the example output before timing
unsigned long startUs = micros();
PORTB ^= (1<<PORTB7);  // Example observable operation
unsigned long elapsedUs = micros() - startUs;
Serial.println(elapsedUs);  // Serial.begin(9600) runs in setup()
```

The exercise does not specify the direction in (iii). Right shift uses `>>`. For (v), subtraction is safe across the unsigned timestamp rollover for intervals shorter than one rollover. At 16 MHz the Arduino Mega's `micros()` advances in 4 µs steps, so repeat a very short operation many times or count CPU cycles to resolve sub-tick times. Keep serial printing outside the measurement.

Assume the two switches provide stable logic levels at PD1:PD0. Read `PIND` and mask its two low bits. Tutorial 12 uses a `switch` statement to take a different action for each input pattern. Apply the same structure here, with `delay()` intervals for the high and low portions on PD7.

| PD1:PD0 | Frequency | Duty cycle | High | Low |
| :---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 00 | 4 Hz | 80% | 200 ms | 50 ms |
| 01 | 50 Hz | 80% | 16 ms | 4 ms |
| 10 | 4 Hz | 50% | 125 ms | 125 ms |
| 11 | 50 Hz | 50% | 10 ms | 10 ms |

The intervals follow $T=1/f$ and $t_{high}=DT$. The software call and GPIO overhead make the values nominal. The selection is sampled at the start of each cycle.

```cpp
#define pulsePin 7

unsigned char z;

void pulse(unsigned int tHigh, unsigned int tLow);

void setup() {
  DDRD &= ~0x03;                  // PD1 and PD0 as inputs
  PORTD &= ~(0x03 | (1<<pulsePin));
  DDRD |= (1<<pulsePin);          // PD7 as output
}

void loop()
{
  z = PIND;
  z &= 0x03;
  switch (z) {
    case 0: pulse(200, 50); break;
    case 1: pulse(16, 4); break;
    case 2: pulse(125, 125); break;
    case 3: pulse(10, 10); break;
  }
}

void pulse(unsigned int tHigh, unsigned int tLow)
{
  PORTD |= (1<<pulsePin);
  delay(tHigh);
  PORTD &= ~(1<<pulsePin);
  delay(tLow);
}
```

This is a software pulse generator derived from the GPIO notes. Timing has normal polling and clock-resolution error. Switch changes take effect on the next cycle.

Assume $f_{\mathrm{CPU}}=16\,\mathrm{MHz}$ and take the pulse from PA4 (Arduino Mega D26), the GPIO used by the notes' CTC example. A 2 Hz, 50% waveform has a period of 0.5 s. Toggle the output every 0.25 s. Use Timer5 in CTC mode, prescaler $N=256$.

$$f_{\rm tick}=16{,}000{,}000/256=62{,}500~\mathrm{Hz},\qquad
\mathrm{OCR5A}=0.25(62{,}500)-1=15{,}624.$$

Timer5 counts from 0 through `OCR5A`, sets the compare flag and restarts the count. Poll and clear the flag on each match. Disable any library that already uses Timer5.

```cpp
#define outPin 4  // PA4

void T5delay();

void setup() {
  PORTA &= ~(1<<outPin);
  DDRA |= (1<<outPin);
  TCCR5B = 0x00;
  TCCR5A = 0x00;
  TIMSK5 = 0x00;
}

void loop()
{
  T5delay();
  PORTA ^= (1<<outPin);
}

void T5delay()
{
  OCR5AH = 0x3D;
  OCR5AL = 0x08;
  TCNT5H = 0x00;
  TCNT5L = 0x00;
  TIFR5 = (1<<OCF5A);  // Clear a pending flag by writing 1
  TCCR5B = 0x0C;        // CTC, clock / 256

  while ((TIFR5 & (1<<OCF5A)) == 0) {}
  TCCR5B = 0x00;
  TIFR5 = (1<<OCF5A);
}
```

Use ADC0 on PF0, Arduino Mega A0, as a single-ended input with common ground. Choose AVCC (nominally 5 V) as the reference and leave the 10-bit result right-adjusted. A 16 MHz oscillator divided by 128 gives $f_{\rm ADC}=125\,\mathrm{kHz}$. A normal conversion takes 13 ADC clocks:

$$t_{\rm conv}=13/(125{,}000)=104\,\mu\mathrm{s}.$$

The first conversion after enabling the ADC takes 25 clocks, about 200 µs. Alignment to the ADC clock, register access and polling add a small amount. The lecture sketch reports about 110 µs for a measured normal conversion. Serial output is excluded from the timed interval and slows the effective loop sampling rate.

```cpp
void setup() {
    DDRF &= ~(1<<PF0);
    PORTF &= ~(1<<PF0);                // No internal pull-up
    ADCSRB = 0x00;                       // MUX5 = 0
    ADMUX = 0x40;                // AVCC, ADC0, right-adjusted
    ADCSRA = 0x87 | (1<<ADIF);      // Enable ADC and clear stale flag
    Serial.begin(9600);
}

void loop() {
    unsigned long startUs = micros();
    ADCSRA |= (1<<ADSC);
    while ((ADCSRA & (1<<ADIF)) == 0) {
        // Wait for a new result.
    }
    unsigned char low = ADCL;
    unsigned char high = ADCH;         // Read after ADCL
    unsigned int sample = ((unsigned int)(high) << 8) | low;
    unsigned long elapsedUs = micros() - startUs;

    ADCSRA = 0x87 | (1<<ADIF);      // Clear completion flag
    Serial.print("ADC = ");
    Serial.print(sample);
    Serial.print(", time = ");
    Serial.print(elapsedUs);
    Serial.println(" us");
}
```

Read bit 5 of **PORTA** at `PA5` (Arduino Mega D27), not Arduino pin D5. Use `PB7` (D13) for an active-high LED and the eight `PORTC` pins for parallel output.

**Synchronization assumption.** The question provides one data line and no clock. Follow the notes' paced-sampling procedure: the source supplies the next bit in each 500 ms LED-off window and holds it through the sampling instant. It can be a manual source responding to the LED or another circuit timed to this pattern. At each sample, shift the accumulated byte right and put the new input in bit 7. After eight samples the first received bit is at bit 0.

```text
PA5 → sampled bit → right-shifting register → PORTC
                               │
                               └── bit/byte indication → PB7 LED
```

```cpp
#define dataMask ((1<<PA5))
#define ledMask ((1<<PB7))

void setup() {
    DDRA &= ~dataMask;
    PORTA &= ~dataMask;   // Well-defined external logic source
    PORTB &= ~ledMask;
    DDRB |= ledMask;
    PORTC = 0;
    DDRC = 0xFF;
}

void loop() {
    unsigned char received = 0;
    for (unsigned char bit = 0; bit < 8; ++bit) {
        PORTB &= ~ledMask;
        delay(500);      // Allow the external source to set its next bit
        received >>= 1;
        if (PINA & dataMask) {
            received |= 0x80;
        }

        PORTB |= ledMask;
        if (bit == 7) {
            PORTC = received;
            delay(5000);  // Extend final bit's indication to 5 s total
        } else {
            delay(2000);
        }
    }
    PORTB &= ~ledMask;
}
```

The final high interval is extended from the normal 2 s to 5 s, as the note's final-byte indication extends its final bit. If the examiner intends a separate 5 s indication, insert a short off gap after the final 2 s pulse and then issue a new 5 s pulse.

Connect active-high LEDs to PB7 (Mega D13) and PA7 (Mega D29), with series resistors for external LEDs. At 16 MHz, the foreground toggles PB7 every 5 ms for a nominal 100 Hz, 50% waveform.

Follow tutorial 16 for the second LED. In Normal mode at prescaler 256, Timer3 counts 62,500 ticks in one second. Load $65536-62500=3036$ (`0x0BDC`) into `TCNT3H:TCNT3L`. On overflow, reload that count and toggle PA7. Each full PA7 blink cycle takes about 2 s. Software delay and ISR reload add small overhead.

```cpp
#include <avr/interrupt.h>

#define mainPin 7       // PB7
#define interruptPin 7  // PA7

void setup() {
  cli();
  PORTB &= ~(1<<mainPin);
  DDRB |= (1<<mainPin);
  PORTA &= ~(1<<interruptPin);
  DDRA |= (1<<interruptPin);

  TCCR3B = 0x00;
  TCCR3A = 0x00;         // Normal mode
  TCNT3H = 0x0B;
  TCNT3L = 0xDC;
  TIFR3 = (1<<TOV3);
  TIMSK3 = (1<<TOIE3);
  TCCR3B = 0x04;         // Clock / 256
  sei();
}

void loop()
{
  delay(5);
  PORTB ^= (1<<mainPin);
}

ISR(TIMER3_OVF_vect)
{
  TCNT3H = 0x0B;
  TCNT3L = 0xDC;
  PORTA ^= (1<<interruptPin);
}
```
